{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:KnotsParticlesConfinement.MagicShellCouplingKappa","@type":"LeanModule","name":"KnotsParticlesConfinement.MagicShellCouplingKappa","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QIntegerCanonical"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.kappaClosed_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.kappaClosed_neg","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L163-L200","doc":"**κ is negative** under the substrate conditions `q > 1, m_e > 0`,\n`0 < hParam q < 1`, and `0 < qBracket q 2 · qBracket q 3`.\n\nThe sign follows structurally: `E₀ > 0` (Hecke energy positive),\n`log(1/h) > 0` since `0 < h < 1` for `q` slightly above `1`, and\nthe q-brackets `[2]_q, [3]_q > 0` for `q > 1`.  The leading\nnegative sign therefore forces `κ_closed < 0` — a property of the\nexpression, asserted of nothing else.\n\nThe `hhParam_lt_one` hypothesis holds for `q ∈ (1, φ)` where\n`φ = (1 + √5)/2` is the golden ratio (the unique positive root\nof `q − q⁻¹ = 1`).  Verified at `q₀ ≈ 1.10998`:\n`h(q₀) ≈ 0.209 < 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.E0_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.E0_pos","kind":"theorem","line":154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L154-L161","doc":"The Hecke energy scale `E₀(q, m_e) > 0` when `m_e > 0` and `q > 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.hParam_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.hParam_pos","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L136-L152","doc":"The Hecke deformation `h(q) = q − q⁻¹` is positive for `q > 1`.\n\nUses the same `inv_mul_cancel₀ + mul_lt_mul'` contradiction\npattern as `QOU.MathlibExt.QPartitionCombinatorics.q_sub_q_inv_pos_at_q_gt_one`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qBracket_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qBracket_zero","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L131-L134","doc":"`[0]_q = 0` by definition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.kappaClosed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.kappaClosed","kind":"def","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L113-L124","doc":"The expression `κ_closed q m_e := −E₀(q, m_e) · log(1/h(q)) / ([2]_q · [3]_q)`.\n\nAt the substrate parameter `q₀ ≈ 1.10998` it evaluates to `−1.0704239`.\n\n**No physical quantity is claimed for this value.**  It was proposed as a\nmagic-shell coupling slope and matched to `−1.0707908`, a number since\nidentified as the Coulomb energy of ³He's pp pair; see the module header.\nThe name is retained because the corpus refers to it, not because the\nidentification stands. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.E0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.E0","kind":"def","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L110-L111","doc":"The Hecke energy scale `E₀ = m_e / (q(q−1))`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.hParam","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.hParam","kind":"def","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L107-L108","doc":"The Hecke deformation parameter `h = q − q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qBracket_normaliser_eq_qFactorial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qBracket_normaliser_eq_qFactorial","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L84-L105","doc":"**The normaliser is a q-factorial, not a two-channel fusion.**\n\n`[2]_q · [3]_q = [3]_q!`.  The recovered producer described this denominator as\n*\"the quantum dimension of `V_2 ⊗ V_3`, the LR fusion of the two relevant\nmagic-shell channels for A = 3 anchors\"*, which reads as a rule about **two**\nchannel indices.  It is a **one**-index object, and one already load-bearing\nelsewhere in the corpus: `F_Bor = |tr_M(β_Bor)| · [3]_q! / q³`, and the q-Schur\nMarkov weight of the trivial partition, `y_{(3)}(q) = q³ / [3]_q!`.  So the\nnormaliser is `q³ / y_{(3)}(q)` — the inverse Markov weight of `(3) ⊢ 3`.\n\nWhy this matters rather than being a notational nicety: a\ntwo-index rule admits several continuations off `A = 3` that all coincide there\n(`(A_magic, A)`, a fixed `(2,3)`, `(A_magic, A_magic+1)`, …), and those spread by\n32× at the 8 shell.  A q-factorial has one index, so the continuation question\nnarrows to which `n` the factorial is taken at — a strictly smaller search, and\none with a categorical handle via the Markov weight.\n\nThe hypothesis is the singular locus, inherited from `[1]_q = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qBracket_eq_canonical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qBracket_eq_canonical","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L70-L82","doc":"**Bridge to the canonical balanced bracket** — and the `n = 0` guard is\nredundant, which is the fact worth recording.  `qIntBal q 0 = (1 − 1)/(q − q⁻¹)`\nis already `0`, so this site agrees with the canonical bracket *everywhere*,\nincluding on the degenerate locus `q − q⁻¹ = 0` where both sides are `0` by\nLean's `x / 0 = 0`.  No hypothesis is needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qBracket","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qBracket","kind":"def","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/magic-shell-coupling-kappa.lean#L64-L68","doc":"The q-bracket `[n]_q = (q^n − q^{-n}) / (q − q⁻¹)` for `n : ℕ`,\nextended via `Real.rpow` for symbolic substrate evaluation. ","signatureChunk":0}]}